WEBVTT

00:00:00.698 --> 00:00:02.341
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> For over a century,

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:06.976
Haida artefacts have formed an important part of the Pitt Rivers collection.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:12.530
This includes this Haida war helmet, with its carved and painted octopus,

00:00:12.641 --> 00:00:16.281
which the Haida believe was worn by prominent chiefs during battle

00:00:16.391 --> 00:00:22.842
or ceremonial war dances. And, of course, there’s the Haida totem pole,

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:25.241
which has become a symbol of the museum.

00:00:25.575 --> 00:00:29.690
It’s over 11 metres tall, the largest object in the collection,

00:00:29.801 --> 00:00:34.354
and shows seated figures and animals such as a bear, a frog, and a raven.

00:00:37.755 --> 00:00:39.805
These totems serve many purposes,

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:44.713
such as commemorating important events or telling the stories of the local people.